Installing dirk
===============

dirk is one statically linkable Rust binary with no runtime dependencies. It
draws on a terminal and spawns programs on pseudo-terminals; nothing else has
to be present for it to run.

This is not the generic GNU installation file, because there is no `configure`
script to describe. A cargo build is what it is, and pretending otherwise would
send a packager looking for options that do not exist.


Requirements
------------

  * Rust 1.88 or newer, with cargo. dirk uses let-chains throughout, which is
    what sets the floor. `rustup` is the usual way to get it:
    <https://rustup.rs>.

  * A terminal that understands 256 colours and, if you want the sidebar to be
    clickable, mouse reporting. Any terminal written this century does.

  * Linux or macOS. Both are tested on every change; nothing else is, which is
    a statement about the tests rather than about portability.

  * `make`, if you want `make install`. `cargo` alone is enough to build.


A release binary
----------------

Releases carry pre-built tarballs for linux and macOS, on both architectures,
at <https://github.com/oddurs/dirk/releases>. Each holds the binary, this file,
the README, NEWS, COPYING and the manual page.

    $ tar -xzf dirk-0.4.0-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz
    $ cd dirk-0.4.0-aarch64-apple-darwin
    $ sudo install -m 755 dirk /usr/local/bin/dirk
    $ sudo install -m 644 dirk.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/dirk.1

Every release also carries SHA256SUMS. Verify before you install:

    $ sha256sum --check --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS


From a source tarball
---------------------

    $ tar -xzf dirk-0.4.0.tar.gz
    $ cd dirk-0.4.0
    $ make
    $ sudo make install

The tarball contains a generated ChangeLog, which the git checkout does not:
the history is the changelog there, and keeping a second copy under version
control only means keeping two.


From a checkout
---------------

    $ git clone https://github.com/oddurs/dirk
    $ cd dirk
    $ make            # cargo build --release; about six seconds
    $ sudo make install

Or without make at all, if you would rather not install anything:

    $ cargo run --release


Where things go
---------------

`make install` honours the two variables a packager needs.

  PREFIX     where the installation is rooted. Default /usr/local.
  DESTDIR    a staging root prepended to everything, for building a package.

    $ make install PREFIX=$HOME/.local
    $ make install DESTDIR=$PWD/pkg PREFIX=/usr

It installs:

  $(PREFIX)/bin/dirk                    the binary
  $(PREFIX)/share/man/man1/dirk.1       the manual page
  $(PREFIX)/share/doc/dirk/             README.md, NEWS, AUTHORS, THANKS, COPYING

BINDIR, MANDIR, DATADIR and DOCDIR override the individual destinations if your
distribution puts them somewhere else.

    $ sudo make uninstall

removes exactly what was installed, and honours the same variables.


Without installing at all
-------------------------

    $ cargo install --git https://github.com/oddurs/dirk

puts the binary in ~/.cargo/bin and nothing else anywhere. There is no crates.io
release: the name `dirk` was taken in 2019 by an unrelated tool, and publishing
under a name that does not match the binary would be worse than not publishing.


Notes for packagers
-------------------

  * The release profile enables LTO, one codegen unit, and symbol stripping. If
    your distribution wants debug symbols, build with
    `cargo build --release --config 'profile.release.strip=false'`.

  * `make dist` produces the source tarball, including the generated ChangeLog.

  * There are no configuration options and nothing is baked in at build time.
    dirk reads ~/.config/dirk/config.toml at runtime, and runs on built-in
    defaults when it is absent.

  * The test suite spawns the real binary on a real pseudo-terminal. It needs
    to be able to allocate one, which usually means /dev/ptmx is present in the
    build environment. `cargo test --lib` skips those if it is not.

  * dirk does not need to be setuid, setgid, or a member of any group. It runs
    entirely as the user who started it.


